1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,145
Hi, I'm Shubhaa.

2
00:00:01,145 --> 00:00:05,189
This is my take-home for Legion
Health: Texas patient onboarding,

3
00:00:05,189 --> 00:00:06,682
rebuilt to be AI-native.

4
00:00:06,682 --> 00:00:11,534
Here, onboarding ends when the first
visit is completed, not when it's booked.

5
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:15,576
Today, of 5,898 sessions
in thirty days, only 23.

6
00:00:15,576 --> 00:00:18,085
8 percent reach the payment page.

7
00:00:18,085 --> 00:00:23,712
Three steps lose 3,376 sessions: the
conditions list, choosing a provider,

8
00:00:23,712 --> 00:00:25,537
and creating an account.

9
00:00:25,537 --> 00:00:31,924
And nothing is tracked after payment, so no
one can see who completes a first visit.

10
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:32,756
Why now?

11
00:00:32,756 --> 00:00:35,662
Lifting start to payment
from 24 to 30 percent

12
00:00:35,662 --> 00:00:39,201
means about 360 more patients
a month reaching checkout,

13
00:00:39,201 --> 00:00:41,097
with no new acquisition spend.

14
00:00:41,097 --> 00:00:45,078
The data vendors are already live,
but intake doesn't use them.

15
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:48,680
And Rula and Grow book in
minutes, with cost shown first.

16
00:00:48,983 --> 00:00:52,984
So the solution is pull, then
confirm, with a human in the loop.

17
00:00:52,984 --> 00:00:56,985
The patient gives a phone number,
date of birth and one consent.

18
00:00:56,985 --> 00:01:01,049
We pull identity, insurance, records
and medications in parallel,

19
00:01:01,049 --> 00:01:03,737
and the patient confirms
instead of typing.

20
00:01:03,737 --> 00:01:06,488
If a pull fails, the
patient simply answers.

21
00:01:06,488 --> 00:01:07,551
No second vendor.

22
00:01:07,883 --> 00:01:12,120
That gives seven screens: the fewest
where each screen does a single job.

23
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:16,996
Safety stays separate, and every step is
measurable, all the way to the first visit.

24
00:01:16,996 --> 00:01:18,156
Let me show it live.

25
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:22,559
The patient enters a phone number and
date of birth, gives one consent,

26
00:01:22,559 --> 00:01:23,821
and types a text code.

27
00:01:23,821 --> 00:01:25,601
That's almost all they'll type.

28
00:01:27,383 --> 00:01:32,568
Screen two is already filled in: name,
address, insurance and a thirty-dollar copay.

29
00:01:32,568 --> 00:01:35,592
The panel on the right
shows each lookup landing.

30
00:01:35,592 --> 00:01:37,567
Patients never see vendor names.

31
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:41,122
Screen three is safety.

32
00:01:41,122 --> 00:01:43,389
It's always asked and never automated.

33
00:01:43,389 --> 00:01:46,730
A yes goes straight to crisis
support, never to booking.

34
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:52,717
On screen four, the patient says
what's going on in their own words.

35
00:01:52,717 --> 00:01:56,573
AI maps it to a fixed list of
concerns, and the patient confirms.

36
00:01:58,917 --> 00:02:02,734
Screen five shows what we pulled,
medications and past records,

37
00:02:02,734 --> 00:02:05,037
for the patient to confirm or correct.

38
00:02:06,983 --> 00:02:09,857
Screen six is Legion's
ranked provider list.

39
00:02:09,857 --> 00:02:12,535
Pick a provider, then a day, then a time.

40
00:02:15,117 --> 00:02:15,981
On screen seven,

41
00:02:15,981 --> 00:02:19,871
each agreement has to be opened and
read to the end before Book unlocks.

42
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:44,601
Then they're booked, get reminder
texts, join the video visit,

43
00:02:44,601 --> 00:02:46,597
and the first visit is completed.

44
00:02:46,597 --> 00:02:47,988
That's the finish line.

45
00:02:57,450 --> 00:03:00,115
When data is missing, the patient answers.

46
00:03:00,115 --> 00:03:03,733
Here no coverage is found, so
self-pay is shown up front.

47
00:03:04,350 --> 00:03:06,971
And here, records haven't
come back in time,

48
00:03:06,971 --> 00:03:09,652
so we ask instead of
making the patient wait.

49
00:03:17,683 --> 00:03:21,387
The key decisions follow one
rule: one source per job.

50
00:03:21,387 --> 00:03:25,708
Verified only for identity, not
Persona as well, to avoid cost,

51
00:03:25,708 --> 00:03:28,314
privacy exposure and conflicting data.

52
00:03:28,314 --> 00:03:31,606
Sohar for insurance, the
only cost source shown.

53
00:03:31,606 --> 00:03:34,418
Metriport for records, in the background.

54
00:03:34,418 --> 00:03:36,133
DoseSpot for medications.

55
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:38,396
And doubt always goes to a human.

56
00:03:38,717 --> 00:03:39,776
Three trade-offs.

57
00:03:39,776 --> 00:03:41,209
Speed versus readiness.

58
00:03:41,209 --> 00:03:45,507
Book-first flows like Rula and Headway
get people into a slot faster.

59
00:03:45,507 --> 00:03:47,937
I put safety and intake before booking,

60
00:03:47,937 --> 00:03:52,049
because the finish line is a
completed first visit, not a booking.

61
00:03:52,049 --> 00:03:55,600
A slot taken by someone who
needs a higher level of care,

62
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,652
or who arrives with no
history, is a wasted slot.

63
00:03:58,652 --> 00:03:59,961
Clicks versus safety.

64
00:03:59,961 --> 00:04:04,882
We pull insurance, meds and records at
every step, but never use them silently.

65
00:04:04,882 --> 00:04:06,377
That costs a few clicks.

66
00:04:06,377 --> 00:04:10,302
In psychiatry a wrong medication
history is a prescribing risk,

67
00:04:10,302 --> 00:04:13,729
so confirmation is the price
of being AI-native safely.

68
00:04:13,729 --> 00:04:15,660
Conversion versus transparency.

69
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:19,211
Showing the copay on screen 2
may lose some people early.

70
00:04:19,211 --> 00:04:22,139
Today they leave at step
11, after ten minutes.

71
00:04:22,139 --> 00:04:24,693
I'd rather they decide in thirty seconds.

72
00:04:24,693 --> 00:04:27,933
If completed visits per
start don't rise, we revert.

73
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:31,148
The north star is start
to first visit completed.

74
00:04:31,148 --> 00:04:34,815
Start to booked is the primary
metric, because it moves first,

75
00:04:34,815 --> 00:04:37,595
and booked to visit
completed is the guardrail,

76
00:04:37,595 --> 00:04:40,079
so faster booking doesn't create no-shows.

77
00:04:40,079 --> 00:04:43,332
Rollout is gated: we stop if
safety or show rate slips.

78
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:44,575
How I built it.

79
00:04:44,575 --> 00:04:49,386
I used Claude to plan: the funnel analysis,
research, the PRD and the designs.

80
00:04:49,386 --> 00:04:53,703
Claude Code then built the repo on
GitHub, wrote tests that run in CI,

81
00:04:53,703 --> 00:04:56,663
deployed to Vercel, and
opened the pull request,

82
00:04:56,663 --> 00:04:59,068
in about 43 minutes of autonomous work.

83
00:04:59,068 --> 00:05:02,768
I stayed in the loop throughout
and made every product call.

84
00:05:03,083 --> 00:05:07,954
Everything is linked here: the live
prototype, the open pull request, the PRD,

85
00:05:07,954 --> 00:05:10,202
the designs and the full AI history.

86
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:11,451
Thanks for watching.
